{
 "manufacturer": "Watlow",
 "manufacturer_slug": "watlow",
 "model": "EZ-ZONE PM PID / limit / integrated controller (Modbus RTU)",
 "model_slug": "ez-zone-pm",
 "short_model": "EZ-ZONE PM",
 "aliases": [
  "EZ-ZONE PM",
  "EZ-ZONE PM Limit",
  "EZ-ZONE PM Integrated"
 ],
 "equipment_type": "Thermostat / temperature controller",
 "applies_to": "Watlow EZ-ZONE PM panel-mount controllers (PID, limit and integrated models) with Modbus RTU, per communications manual 0600-0056-0000 Rev. B (Nov 2007).",
 "summary": "The EZ-ZONE PM is Watlow's panel-mount PID and limit controller family. With the Modbus RTU option every parameter has a fixed register: floats take two registers and enumerated settings use Watlow's numeric codes (for example 106 = Yes). This map covers the operations values an integrator needs: input process values and errors, loop set points, control mode, PID terms, output power, autotune, event and limit states, alarm 1 set points and status, and the basic communications settings. The full parameter list is in the manual.",
 "transport": [
  "RTU"
 ],
 "defaults": {
  "unit_id": 1,
  "baud": null,
  "parity": "None",
  "data_bits": 8,
  "stop_bits": 1,
  "tcp_port": null
 },
 "addressing": {
  "convention": "0-based",
  "note": "The manual lists 'relative' Modbus registers (less the 40,001 offset) and sends them unchanged: the worked read of analog input 1 sends 360 as hex 01 68. In a Modicon-style tool, 360 is 40361."
 },
 "default_word_order": "CDAB",
 "firmware": null,
 "source": {
  "title": "EZ-ZONE PM Controller Communications Manual",
  "doc_number": "0600-0056-0000 Rev. B",
  "revision_date": "2007-11",
  "url": "https://www.instrumart.com/assets/ezzone-pm_communicationsmanual.pdf",
  "pages": "pp. 11-13 (32-bit read example and frame), pp. 56-61 (operations page), p. 85 (communications menu)",
  "unofficial": true
 },
 "notes": [
  "Watlow does not appear to host this communications manual publicly; the copy linked is a distributor (Instrumart) copy of the unaltered Watlow manual, Rev. B from 2007.",
  "Floats are IEEE 754 in two registers with the low word first by default (CDAB): register 360 holds the low word and 361 the high word, as the manual's example shows. The order can be changed to high-low in the Communications menu; this map assumes the default.",
  "Integers are unsigned 16-bit. Enumerated parameters use Watlow codes, e.g. 59 = No, 106 = Yes, 62 = Off.",
  "Temperatures follow the display units chosen for the communications port (°F by default).",
  "Only a subset is listed (operations-page values, alarm 1 and comms basics); alarms 2-4 follow the same pattern at other registers given in the manual, as do setup and profile parameters.",
  "Left out: the word-order and non-volatile-save registers, which the manual prints with the same address (2494); the baud-rate register, whose value codes are not printed. The manual gives the default serial format as 9,600 baud in its footnotes but lists 38,400 as the baud-rate default, so the default baud is left blank."
 ],
